            <ArticleTitle>Severe central sleep apnea in a child with leukemia on chronic methadone therapy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>85-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/ppul.22539</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We describe a child with acute myeloid leukemia (AML) who developed severe central sleep apnea (CSA) on methadone therapy for chronic pain management. His chemotherapy-related cerebral atrophy and renal insufficiency with impaired methadone clearance may have also contributed to the severity of his sleep-disordered breathing. Maintenance methadone treatment is not a common pediatric practice; therefore, the adverse effects of methadone therapy, including CSA, are rarely reported in children.</AbstractText>
